{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"AC6 cachePoint TTL — STATED WITH EVIDENCE, and it CORRECTS THIS TICKETS OWN PREMISE.\n\nThe description asserts \"Bedrocks cachePoint carries NO TTL where Anthropics cache_control\ncarries ttl:5m\" and calls that difference substantive. That premise is WRONG, and the correction is\nthe answer to the criterion:\n\n  Bedrocks cachePoint block takes an OPTIONAL `ttl` that DEFAULTS TO 5 MINUTES — the same default\n  Anthropics cache_control applies. The TTL is not missing on Bedrock; it is defaulted, and\n  defaulted to the SAME value. rebar emits no explicit TTL on either path, so both yield a 5-minute\n  lifetime. There is no observable difference to account for, and no per-provider cache tuning is\n  needed.\n  Bedrock additionally offers a 1-hour TTL for some Claude models. It is opt-in and rebar does not\n  request it, so it is not a difference either.\n  Sources: AWS \"Amazon Bedrock now supports 1-hour duration for prompt caching\" (Jan 2026) and the\n  Bedrock cachePoint API reference (ttl optional, valid \"5m\"/\"1h\", default 5m).\n\nEMPIRICAL EVIDENCE, from my own three Bedrock gate runs (cache READS, not writes — a write-only\ntally is bug f81ds failure mode and proves nothing):\n  review-plan       cache_read 226,668 across 18 of 27 calls\n  review-code       cache_read  18,292\n  verify-completion cache_read  45,122\nCaching demonstrably ENGAGES on the Bedrock path on all three gates.\n\nHONEST LIMIT, stated rather than papered over: no gate run exercises a gap LONGER than 5 minutes\nbetween calls, because each gate completes well inside one TTL window. So the runs confirm the\nlifetime is not shorter than a gate run; they do not probe expiry. That does not affect the\ncriterion, which asks whether the TTL difference causes an observable difference — there is no\ndifference in the defaults to cause one.\n\nRECORDED IN THE DELIVERABLE: docs/local-dev-env.md, new bullet in the Bedrock sections \"Things\nthat bite\" list, carrying the default-parity statement and the three measured cache_read figures.\n\nAC7 REVERT — EXECUTED AND PROVEN, token-free in BOTH directions via load_class_slots/resolve_class:\n  export REBAR_LLM_CONFIG_FILE=~/.config/rebar/bedrock.toml ->\n    {trivial: bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-haiku-4-5-20251001-v1:0,\n     standard: bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6,\n     frontier: bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8}\n  unset REBAR_LLM_CONFIG_FILE ->\n    {trivial: anthropic:claude-haiku-4-5,\n     standard: anthropic:claude-sonnet-4-6,\n     frontier: anthropic:claude-opus-4-8}\nOne variable, all three classes flip back to Anthropic. A developer gets back in one step.\n\nAC8 infra/ UNTOUCHED — verified by diff, not by assertion:\n  git diff --name-only origin/main..HEAD -- infra/  =>  0 files."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785768976747239001,"uuid":"c8957006-e70f-4c26-8f96-73fbb270fef0"}